{
  "id": "Compound::DB01124 - Gene::3767",
  "verbalization": "Tolbutamide is linked to Kcnj11 through a series of molecular interactions that suggest a potential role in modulating potassium channel activity. The evidence indicates that Tolbutamide binds to Kcnj1, which is a component of the Atp-activated Inward Rectifier Potassium Channel, and this channel is subsequently participated by Kcnj11. This suggests a pathway where Tolbutamide may influence the function of Kcnj11 through its interaction with Kcnj1, potentially altering the activity of the potassium channel. Additionally, the inclusion of Tolbutamide in sulfonylurea compounds, which are known to interact with Kcnj11, further supports a direct or indirect relationship between Tolbutamide and Kcnj11. The shared classification under sulfonylurea antidiabetic agents highlights a common pharmacological context, reinforcing the possibility of a functional link. The integration of these pathways suggests that Tolbutamide may exert its effects on Kcnj11 through a mechanism involving potassium channel modulation, though the exact nature of this interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
